  ],
  "text" : "A good LLM wiki needs four layers.\nA place where agents can write for agents, a clean surface for humans to read, a curated\nlayer of slowly changing context and rules that you review, and a janitor that keeps\nit all up to date and consistent.\nThe agent layer is written by agents for other agents.\nAgents can be as verbose as they need to be.\nThis can include details that they need to resume a task later, like hashes, branches,\ncommits.\nYou never need to read it or go here.\nThe human layer is the clean, distilled view that you link out and that goes to these detailed\nagent notes when necessary.\nThink of a dashboard or a Kanban board.\nIt's less about reviewing what the agent's up to and more about managing your own context.\nThe curated layer, this is the slowly changing context that you need to handle with care.\nThese are the skills and rules, the conventions of your repo.\nAnd then you need the janitor.\nThis looks to catch conflicting rules in your agent notes before they spread.\nAnd it cleans up old material so the human layer stays useful.\nThis is how I structure all of my repos these days.\nWith all four layers, you can build a strong, agentic knowledge base."
